      "text": "ng latency by using the Places API Place Autocomplete service to obtain place IDs, then pass the Place IDs to the Directions API (Legacy) or Distance Matrix API (Legacy). Respond to user input Applications that respond in real-time to user input have two major considerations that affect the choice of API: User input generally involves entering an address progressively (such as \"123 Main Street\"), so being able to geocode incomplete, ambiguous addresses is beneficial as it lets the user get a result faster. Applications that respond to user input are highly latency-sensitive. These two considerations make the Place Autocomplete service in the Places API ideal for the use case of responding to user input. Place Autocomplete is designed to return multiple possible options and to allow a user to choose between them. The Places API can be restricted to search only geocodes or addresses while excluding businesses. Further, the autocomplete lookup function can be biased to return results specific to a location. The Places API returns a place ID which can be passed as a fully disambiguated location to the Geocoding API web service, which then returns full address details, and geocodes the address into a latlng. Place IDs can also be passed to other APIs, such as the Directions API (Legacy) and Distance Matrix API (Legacy) (see Reduce latency ). Address geocoding in the Geocoding API has much higher latency, and also produces less accurate results for incomplete or ambiguous queries, so it isn't recommended for applications that have to respond in real time to user input. Get more information on the Place Autocomplete service for Android , iOS , JavaScript , and the Places API . A"
    },
    {
      "term": "ambiguous",
      "text": "toolkit Code Assist toolkit Maps Experience agent tools Maps in AI Studio Additional Resources API Security Best Practices Digital Signature Guide Map Coverage Details Optimization Guide Mobile OS and software support Launch stages Legacy products Deprecations URL Encoding GitHub YouTube LinkedIn Discord Innovators Issue Tracker Geocoding API v4 methods have a default quota of 25 queries per second (QPS). For information on requesting a higher quota, see View and manage quotas and select Geocoding API as the service. Home Products Google Maps Platform Documentation Geocoding API Resources Send feedback Geocoding Addresses Best Practices Stay organized with collections Save and categorize content based on your preferences. Page Summary outlined_flag Geocoding converts addresses into geographical coordinates (latitude and longitude) for map placement. Use the Geocoding API for complete, unambiguous addresses and the Places API Place Autocomplete service for ambiguous addresses or latency-sensitive applications. For real-time user input, prioritize the Places API Place Autocomplete service due to its ability to handle incomplete addresses and low latency. The Directions and Distance Matrix APIs benefit from using Place IDs obtained from the Places API Place Autocomplete service to reduce latency when working with addresses. Geocoding is the process of converting addresses (like a street address) into geographic coordinates (latitude and longitude) that you can use to place markers on a map, or position the map. The focus of this document is to clarify considerations involved when geocoding addresses. It describes when it's optimal to use the Geocoding API and when it's benef"
    },
    {
      "term": "Calendar",
      "text": "not found"
    }
  ]
}

